BTB = "This lesson summarises ideas from Better Than Before by Gretchen Rubin in our own words."
L = {}
L['btb-monitoring'] = dict(
intro="It is surprisingly hard to change something you are not paying attention to. Better Than Before describes the Strategy of Monitoring: keeping track of a behaviour so that you see it clearly. This lesson explores why simply noticing can shift habits, and how to monitor without becoming obsessive. Where money is mentioned, this is general learning only, not financial advice. " + BTB,
sections=[
("We underestimate what we do not track", "Many people, if asked how much time they spend on their phone, how much they spend on snacks, or how many steps they walk, give answers that turn out to be quite far from reality once they actually measure. The book points out that our impressions of our own behaviour are often unreliable. Monitoring replaces guesswork with information, which makes it easier to decide what, if anything, to change."),
("Noticing changes behaviour", "Simply tracking something can nudge it in a better direction. When you write down every purchase, you may pause before an impulse buy because you know you will have to record it. When you note what you eat, you may become more aware of mindless snacking. Monitoring creates a moment of attention, and attention gives you a chance to choose."),
("What to monitor", "It helps to monitor only what matters to you right now, rather than tracking everything. Choose one or two behaviours connected to a habit you want to change: hours of sleep, minutes of movement, money spent on eating out, or time on social media. Keep the method simple: a notebook, a note on your phone, or a built-in screen-time report. The easier it is, the more likely you are to keep it up."),
("Monitoring money", "Money is one area where monitoring often brings clarity. Looking back at a month of UPI payments or card statements and grouping them into a few categories can reveal surprising patterns. The aim is awareness, not judgement. Any decisions about budgeting, saving or investing depend on your own circumstances, and a qualified professional can help with larger choices. This is not financial advice."),
("Avoiding the trap of over-tracking", "Monitoring can become counterproductive if it turns into anxiety or perfectionism. If tracking makes you feel worse rather than more aware, simplify it, track less often, or pause it altogether. The goal is useful information, not constant self-surveillance. It is fine to monitor for a few weeks to learn, then stop once the habit is steady."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the screen-time surprise", "Meenakshi, a schoolteacher in Madurai, felt she used her phone 'only a little' in the evenings. Out of curiosity, she checked her phone's built-in screen-time report for a week. The total was much higher than she expected, mostly short-video apps after dinner. She did not set strict limits; she simply looked at the report each Sunday. Over a month, her evening use dropped as she became more aware, and she started reading with her daughter instead. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Our impressions of our own behaviour are often inaccurate.","Tracking creates a moment of attention that can shift behaviour.","Monitor one or two things that matter, using a simple method.","Stop or simplify if tracking causes anxiety. Not financial advice."],
reflect=["Which behaviour do you suspect you underestimate?","What is the simplest way you could track it for one week?"],
)
L['btb-abstainer'] = dict(
intro="When trying to cut back on something tempting, some people do best by giving it up entirely, while others do best by allowing a little. Better Than Before calls these two styles Abstainers and Moderators. This lesson explains both and how knowing your style can make habits easier. " + BTB,
sections=[
("Two ways of handling temptation", "The book observes that people respond to temptation in different ways. Abstainers find it easier to have none of something than a little. Once they start, they find it hard to stop, and constant decisions about 'how much' wear them out. Moderators find that strict rules make them feel deprived and rebellious; having a little now and then satisfies them and helps them stay on track."),
("The Abstainer's experience", "For an Abstainer, a rule like 'no sweets on weekdays' can actually feel freeing. Instead of debating every time sweets appear, the answer is simply no. The book suggests that Abstainers often feel relief rather than deprivation once they decide firmly. Trying to moderate, by contrast, can lead to endless bargaining: 'Just one more. Well, it's already open. I'll start tomorrow.'"),
("The Moderator's experience", "For a Moderator, the same strict rule might make sweets seem even more desirable. Knowing they can have a small piece after dinner makes it easier to skip the rest. Moderators tend to do well with flexibility, and an absolute ban can backfire, leading to overindulgence once the rule is broken."),
("Finding your style", "To discover your style, think about past experiences. When you tried to cut back on something, did having a little satisfy you, or did it open the door to having a lot? Did a firm 'never' feel like relief or like a cage? Your style might differ between areas of life; you might be an Abstainer with chips but a Moderator with television. The book stresses that neither style is better; they are just different."),
("Respecting others' styles", "Knowing about these two styles can also reduce friction with family and friends. An Abstainer may not want a 'tiny taste' offered repeatedly. A Moderator may resent being told to give something up entirely. Respecting each person's approach makes support more effective."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the namkeen packet", "Gaurav, a bank clerk in Kanpur, loved salty namkeen. He kept trying to eat 'just a handful' in the evenings, but often finished the whole packet. When he read about Abstainers and Moderators, he recognised that he was probably an Abstainer for this snack. He stopped buying it for home and kept it only for festivals. To his surprise, he felt calmer, not deprived, because he was no longer negotiating with himself every night. His wife, a Moderator, kept enjoying a small bowl now and then. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Abstainers find it easier to have none than a little.","Moderators find that a little now and then keeps them on track.","Neither style is better; find out which suits you in each area.","Respect others' styles when offering support."],
reflect=["When you allow yourself a little of something tempting, what usually happens next?","Is there an area where a firm rule might actually feel like relief?"],
)
L['btb-loopholes'] = dict(
intro="We are remarkably creative at finding reasons to skip our own habits. Better Than Before describes the Strategy of Loophole-Spotting: learning to recognise the excuses we make, so we can catch them in the moment. This lesson walks through common loopholes and how to respond. Where money is mentioned, this is general learning only, not financial advice. " + BTB,
sections=[
("What a loophole is", "A loophole is a justification that lets us off the hook from a habit we have chosen. It often sounds reasonable in the moment. 'I've been so good all week, I deserve this.' 'It's a special occasion.' 'I'll start fresh on Monday.' The book suggests that by naming common types of loopholes, we can spot them more easily and decide whether we really want to use them."),
("Common types", "The book lists several categories. Moral licensing: I've been good, so I've earned a reward that undoes my effort. Tomorrow logic: it's fine to skip today because I'll do it tomorrow. Lack of control: I had no choice. Planning to fail: putting yourself in a tempting situation 'by accident'. This doesn't count: holidays, weekends or travel somehow don't count. Questionable assumption: 'I'm too busy'. Concern for others: 'I don't want to be rude by refusing'. Fake self-actualisation: 'You only live once'. One-coin loophole: 'one time won't matter'."),
("Catching loopholes in action", "Spotting a loophole does not mean you must never use it. It means you notice when you are reaching for one and make a conscious choice. Some people find it helpful to say the loophole out loud or write it down: 'I'm using tomorrow logic.' Naming it often takes away some of its persuasive power. Sometimes you will decide the exception is worth it; that is your choice to make."),
("Planning for predictable exceptions", "Many loopholes appear at predictable times: weddings, festivals, travel, exam weeks. Instead of pretending these will not happen, you can plan for them. Decide in advance how you want to handle Diwali sweets, a family wedding, or a work trip. A planned exception is different from a loophole because you chose it calmly, not in the heat of the moment."),
("Loopholes and money", "Loopholes are common in spending too: 'It's on sale, so I'm saving money', or 'It's only a small amount'. Noticing these thoughts can help you decide more deliberately. What counts as reasonable spending depends on your own situation. This is not financial advice."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the 'it's a sale' loophole", "Tanvi, a young professional in Pune, wanted to cut back on online clothes shopping. During a big online sale, she found herself thinking, 'If I buy now, I'm actually saving money.' She recognised this as a loophole she had used many times. She paused, wrote it in her notes app, and asked whether she would buy the item at full price. The answer was no, so she closed the app. She did buy one thing she had planned for months, which felt like a choice rather than a slip. This is an illustrative story and not financial advice."),
takeaways=["Loopholes are justifications that let us skip our own habits.","Common types include moral licensing, tomorrow logic and 'this doesn't count'.","Naming a loophole helps you choose deliberately.","Plan for predictable exceptions in advance. Not financial advice."],
reflect=["Which loophole do you use most often?","What predictable occasion coming up could you plan for now?"],
)
L['btb-convenience'] = dict(
intro="One of the simplest ways to change behaviour is to change how easy or hard it is. Better Than Before describes the Strategies of Convenience and Inconvenience: making good habits easier to do and unwanted habits harder. This lesson explores practical ways to adjust the effort involved in everyday choices. " + BTB,
sections=[
("Effort shapes choices", "The book observes that people are strongly influenced by convenience. We tend to do what is easiest. If healthy food takes effort to prepare but packaged snacks are ready to eat, we often reach for the snacks. If the gym is far away but the sofa is right here, the sofa often wins. Rather than fighting this tendency, the strategies use it: make the good choice the easy one."),
("Making good habits convenient", "Convenience can be added in many small ways. Keep a filled water bottle on your desk. Chop vegetables in advance so cooking is quicker. Lay out workout clothes the night before. Keep a book in your bag for waiting moments. Each step removes a small barrier, and small barriers add up. The book suggests looking for any point where a good habit stalls and asking what would make it easier."),
("Making unwanted habits inconvenient", "The reverse also works. To reduce a habit, add a little effort. Keep snacks on a high shelf or in a closed box. Log out of social media apps so you have to sign in each time. Leave your card at home when browsing shops. Unplug the television after use. These small inconveniences create a pause, and in that pause you can choose differently."),
("Paying for convenience wisely", "Sometimes making a good habit convenient involves spending, such as joining a gym close to home or buying a simple kitchen tool. The book acknowledges that this can be worthwhile if it removes a real barrier. Whether it is right for you depends on your situation and budget. This is not financial advice."),
("Being kind to yourself", "These strategies are not about tricking yourself or creating harsh rules. They are about designing a life where the choices you value are the natural, easy ones. Experiment, see what works, and adjust as you go."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the fruit on the table", "In Sanjay's household in Surat, biscuits lived in an open tin on the dining table and fruit lived in the fridge's bottom drawer. Everyone snacked on biscuits. His wife suggested a swap: a bowl of washed fruit on the table and the biscuit tin on a high shelf. Nobody banned biscuits, but within a couple of weeks the family was eating more fruit simply because it was the first thing they saw and could reach. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["People tend to choose what is easiest.","Make good habits convenient by removing small barriers.","Make unwanted habits inconvenient by adding small effort.","Design your surroundings so valued choices are the easy ones."],
reflect=["Which good habit stalls because of one small barrier?","What tiny inconvenience could you add to an unwanted habit?"],
)
L['btb-clarity'] = dict(
intro="It is hard to build a habit when you are not sure what you want or why. Better Than Before describes the Strategy of Clarity: being clear about your values and about the specific actions you will take. This lesson explores how clarity reduces inner conflict and makes habits easier to keep. Where money is mentioned, this is general learning only, not financial advice. " + BTB,
sections=[
("Two kinds of clarity", "The book distinguishes between clarity of values and clarity of action. Clarity of values means understanding what matters to you and why. Clarity of action means knowing exactly what you will do. Both are important. Without clear values, habits can feel pointless or conflicting. Without clear actions, good intentions stay vague and never turn into behaviour."),
("Conflicting values", "Sometimes habits stall because two values pull in different directions. You may value both rest and productivity, both saving money and enjoying life, both time with family and career progress. When values conflict, it is easy to feel stuck or guilty whichever way you choose. Naming the conflict openly helps. You can then decide how to balance it, for example by setting specific times for each, rather than feeling torn every day."),
("Vague versus specific", "'Be healthier' or 'spend less' are hard to act on because they are not clear about what to do. 'Walk for twenty minutes after dinner' or 'check expenses every Sunday evening' are much easier. The book suggests that the more concrete a habit is, the easier it is to start and to know whether you have done it. Specific habits also make it easier to notice when you are slipping."),
("Asking 'why' and 'how'", "A helpful exercise is to ask 'why' several times about a habit, to reach the value underneath. Why do I want to save? To feel secure. Why does that matter? So I can worry less about family emergencies. Then ask 'how' to turn that value into an action: set aside a small amount on payday. Any savings approach should fit your own circumstances. This is not financial advice."),
("Revisiting clarity", "Values and priorities change over time: a new job, a new child, a move, or a health change can shift what matters. It is worth revisiting your clarity now and then, and adjusting habits to fit who you are now rather than who you were."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: rest versus side-project", "Harish, a software developer in Bengaluru, felt guilty every weekend. If he rested, he felt lazy for not working on his side project. If he worked, he felt exhausted on Monday. When he thought about his values clearly, he realised both rest and creativity mattered. He made a concrete plan: Saturday morning for the project, the rest of the weekend for rest and family, without guilt. The inner tug-of-war eased. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Clarity of values and clarity of action both support habits.","Conflicting values can cause inner tension; name them and decide how to balance.","Specific habits are easier to act on than vague intentions.","Revisit your values as life changes. Not financial advice."],
reflect=["Which two of your values sometimes pull against each other?","How could you make one vague intention more specific this week?"],
)
L['btb-scheduling'] = dict(
intro="Many good intentions fail not because we do not care but because we never set a time for them. Better Than Before describes the Strategy of Scheduling: giving a habit a specific, regular time so that it happens without daily debate. This lesson explores how scheduling works and how to make it flexible enough for real life. Where money is mentioned, this is general learning only, not financial advice. " + BTB,
sections=[
("Why scheduling helps", "When a habit has a set time, you no longer need to decide whether and when to do it each day. The decision is made once. The book observes that this can be a great relief, especially for people who find daily decisions draining. A scheduled habit also signals importance: if it is on the calendar, it matters."),
("Choosing the right time", "The best time for a habit depends on your energy, responsibilities and personality. Some people are sharper in the morning and prefer to do important habits early before the day gets busy. Others are evening people. Think about when you have energy and when interruptions are least likely. A habit scheduled at a realistic time is far more likely to survive than one squeezed into an impossible slot."),
("Scheduling for money tasks", "Money tasks, such as reviewing expenses, paying bills or checking savings, are often postponed because they feel uncomfortable. Putting them on the calendar, for example a short review every Sunday evening or on the first of each month, can make them routine rather than a source of dread. The specific approach should suit your own situation. This is not financial advice."),
("Scheduling time for yourself", "The book also suggests scheduling things that are easy to neglect because they feel optional: rest, hobbies, time with friends. If only work and chores make it onto the calendar, there may be little room left for what refreshes you. Protecting a slot for something you enjoy is a way of taking your own wellbeing seriously."),
("Flexibility and recovery", "Schedules will sometimes break: illness, travel, guests, emergencies. A schedule is a tool, not a test. When a slot is missed, the key is to return to it at the next opportunity rather than abandon the habit. Some people find it helpful to have a back-up slot for important habits."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the Sunday money slot", "Aisha, a freelance writer in Lucknow, dreaded looking at her finances and kept postponing it. Her payments came irregularly, and she often felt anxious. She added a recurring thirty-minute slot to her calendar every Sunday at five, with tea, to note income, expenses and upcoming bills. At first she resisted, but having a set time meant she did not have to keep deciding. After a couple of months, the anxiety eased because she always knew roughly where she stood. This is an illustrative story and not financial advice."),
takeaways=["Scheduling turns a daily decision into a one-time choice.","Choose times that match your energy and responsibilities.","Schedule uncomfortable tasks and enjoyable ones alike.","When a slot is missed, return to it next time. Not financial advice."],
reflect=["Which habit keeps slipping because it has no set time?","What enjoyable activity deserves a protected slot in your week?"],
)
